## Notes for the prompt author

(Delete this section before committing.)

A good iOS slash prompt:
- Used at least once a day (otherwise upfront design cost doesn't pay back)
- Has hard NOs matching project's golden rules
- Names each step explicitly so user can interrupt cleanly
- Never destructively modifies state without explicit per-call confirmation

A bad iOS slash prompt:
- Wraps a one-step workflow (just type the command directly)
- Reads contextual state inside prompt body (slow — use Step 1 to gather first)
- Auto-confirms destructive operations
- Re-implements features Copilot already has (e.g. summarizing the diff — Copilot can do that natively)
